  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule are both created, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Fields:** the Kibana Sample Data Logs data view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`) holds web-request records. The key fields are:\n- **Time:** `timestamp`\n- **Request:** `request.keyword`, `url.keyword`, `extension.keyword`, `referer`\n- **Response:** `response.keyword`, `bytes`\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `agent.keyword`, `machine.os.keyword`\n- **Geography:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-a32e9be6 Web logs overview\" (id `26dd765d-e1aa-43cb-988e-f51085a1b7fc`) has three panels:\n- Total requests, a count metric.\n- Average bytes, an average of `bytes`.\n- Requests over time, a line chart built from an ES|QL query.\n\nI haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana, so I haven't checked how the panels render.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-a32e9be6 5xx spike\" (id `ab28589a-fe4d-4b88-bfff-974eccecc2bc`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every 1 minute. It counts documents in a 5-minute window where `response.keyword` starts with `5`, and fires when the count is above 10. It has no actions attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.",
